window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"][5]={"university_computer_science_en:M5-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M5-L1 – Where does artificial intelligence come from?<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor’s: AI fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: Not one invention, but several research\ntraditions<\/h2>\n<p>Modern artificial intelligence did not emerge from a single\ngroundbreaking discovery. It is the result of several ideas pursued over\ndecades: machines should <strong>reason using rules<\/strong>,\n<strong>learn from examples<\/strong>, <strong>search large sets of\npossible solutions<\/strong> or <strong>recognise patterns in\nperception<\/strong>. Initially, these approaches often existed alongside\none another or competed with each other. Only later did systems emerge\nthat combined several methods.<\/p>\n<p>The present-day impression that artificial intelligence is primarily\na chat window is therefore a considerable historical simplification.\nChess programs, medical expert systems, image recognition, search\nengines, robotics, statistics and language processing are also part of\nthe history of the field. Many AI systems have no conversational\ninterface at all.<\/p>\n<p>The central question of this lesson is: <strong>What technical\nproblems did researchers have to solve in succession before powerful\nlanguage, image and AI assistance systems became possible?<\/strong> The\nfollowing lessons M5-L2–M6-L7 then examine the technology of these\nsystems in detail.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What does artificial intelligence mean?<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Artificial intelligence (AI)<\/strong> is a field of research\nand application in which computer systems are intended to implement\ncapabilities associated with intelligent human behaviour: recognising\npatterns, processing language, preparing decisions, solving problems or\nlearning from experience, for example. This is a working definition, not\na universally valid test of consciousness or human understanding.<\/p>\n<p>We should distinguish five terms from the outset:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term<\/th>\n<th>Basic idea<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Symbolic AI<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Process knowledge using explicitly formulated rules and symbols<\/td>\n<td>Expert system with if-then rules<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Machine learning<\/strong> (ML)<\/td>\n<td>Derive rules or patterns statistically from example data rather than\nprogramming every case by hand<\/td>\n<td>Detection of unwanted email<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deep learning<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Machine learning using neural networks, usually with multiple\nlayers<\/td>\n<td>Recognition of objects in images<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Generative AI<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Generate new text, images, sounds or other content<\/td>\n<td>A language model writes a report<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Large language model<\/strong> (LLM)<\/td>\n<td>Process and generate language/token sequences with an extensively\ntrained model<\/td>\n<td>A conversational language model<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>These terms are <strong>not alternatives at the same level<\/strong>.\nDeep learning is part of machine learning; many modern language models\nuse deep learning and can operate generatively. Symbolic methods, in\nturn, can be used together with an LLM in an application.<\/p>\n<p>A simple sketch of their relationships:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Artificial intelligence (AI)\n│\n├── Symbolic methods\n│   └── Rules, logic, planning, search\n│\n├── Machine learning (ML)\n│   ├── Classical statistical methods\n│   └── Neural networks / deep learning\n│       ├── Image and audio models\n│       └── Language models, including many LLMs\n│\n└── Combinations\n    └── e.g. language model + tool call + fixed safety rules<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The sketch shows research directions, not a complete taxonomy with\nstrictly defined boundaries. In particular, search and planning methods\ncan be combined with learning.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Why is the distinction useful?<\/strong> It prevents us from\nmistaking a rigid rule-based program and a data-driven neural network\nfor the same technical principle. <strong>When is it secondary?<\/strong>\nFor the user of a simple file filter, correct operation matters first;\nan AI component is not useful simply because it sounds more modern.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The background: Logic, computing machines and the artificial\nneuron<\/h2>\n<p>Long before people spoke of artificial intelligence, mathematics and\nlogic investigated whether thinking could at least partly be expressed\nin rules. Formal logic describes which statements follow from which\npremises. Computer science later showed how such rules could be\nprocessed by machines.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"31-1943-mcculloch-und-pitts\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>1943 – McCulloch and Pitts<\/h3>\n<p>Warren McCulloch and Walter Pitts published <em>A Logical Calculus of\nthe Ideas Immanent in Nervous Activity<\/em> in 1943. They proposed\nmodelling simple neuron-like units as logical circuits. In the model,\nthe output of a unit depends on its inputs and a specified rule.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> This was a theoretical model, not a\nmodern language model capable of learning. The main achievement was to\nbring biological inspiration and formal computability together. Today’s\nartificial neuron is likewise <strong>not a complete representation of a\nbiological neuron<\/strong>, but a highly simplified mathematical\ncomputational unit.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Input A ──┐\n          ├──&gt; formal neuron model ──&gt; output\nInput B ──┘       (rule/threshold)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Later developments added, in particular, adjustable connection\nweights and learning methods. M5-L2 explains the numerical calculations\nin such an artificial neuron.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"32-1950-alan-turing-verschiebt-die-frage\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>1950 – Alan Turing\nreframes the question<\/h3>\n<p>Alan Turing published the paper <em>Computing Machinery and\nIntelligence<\/em> in 1950. Rather than becoming stuck on the difficult\ndefinition of the word ‘thinking’, he proposed the <strong>Imitation\nGame<\/strong>: a machine is considered through its linguistically\nmediated responses and the judgement of a human interlocutor.<\/p>\n<p>In a simplified form, this later became known as the <strong>Turing\ntest<\/strong>. Care is needed here: Turing proposed a particular way of\nstudying machine behaviour. <strong>A successful dialogue proves neither\nconsciousness nor general reliability, factual accuracy or human\nunderstanding.<\/strong> Conversely, failing a dialogue test does not\nmean that the system is useless for other tasks.<\/p>\n<p>The historical significance is that machine behaviour can be tested\nexperimentally instead of arguing exclusively about philosophical\nconcepts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>1955/1956 – Dartmouth and the founding of a research\nfield<\/h2>\n<p>On <strong>31 August 1955<\/strong>, John McCarthy, Marvin Minsky,\nNathaniel Rochester and Claude Shannon formulated the proposal for the\n<em>Dartmouth Summer Research Project on Artificial Intelligence<\/em>.\nThe joint research meeting took place in <strong>1956<\/strong>. Through\nthis undertaking, the term <em>Artificial Intelligence<\/em> became\nestablished as the name of a distinct research programme.<\/p>\n<p>The original proposal was remarkably ambitious: language, concept\nformation, problem-solving and the ability of machines to improve\nthemselves were to be investigated. The authors believed considerable\nprogress could be achieved in a short time. In retrospect, this\nillustrates a recurring tension in AI research: a goal that is plausible\nin principle can be technically much harder than initially assumed.<\/p>\n<p>Early researchers were interested not only in artificial neurons.\n<strong>Symbolic logic, search methods and knowledge\nrepresentation<\/strong> also played a central role.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Mathematics + logic + information processing\n                    │\n                    ▼\n            AI research programme\n                    │\n       ┌────────────┴────────────┐\n       ▼                         ▼\nSymbolic programs            Learning models\n(Rules, proofs, search)     (Patterns from data)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The division into two branches is for teaching purposes: from the\noutset there were overlaps, other approaches and different conceptions\nof ‘intelligence’.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two competing basic ideas: Rules or learning?<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"51-symbolische-ki-das-wissen-ausdruecklich-aufschreiben\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Symbolic\nAI – Writing knowledge down explicitly<\/h3>\n<p>A symbolic system stores concepts, facts and rules in a form that a\nprogram can process directly. Example: an internal document processing\nrule states: <em>If a file has property X and a particular access\noperation is permitted, it may be assigned to category Y.<\/em> The\nsystem checks the conditions and applies the rule.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Problem this approach solves:<\/strong> Clear rules can be\nunderstandable, verifiable and reproducible. Where laws, business rules\nor defined technical states are decisive, this clarity is often a major\nadvantage.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> Anyone wishing to handle arbitrary\nimages, colloquial expressions or almost infinitely many exceptional\nsituations cannot list every meaningful rule in advance. Even small\nchanges in the world can make large sets of rules difficult to\nmaintain.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"52-lernen-das-verhalten-aus-beispielen-ableiten\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning –\nDeriving behaviour from examples<\/h3>\n<p>A learning system receives example material and a learning method\nthat adjusts internal numerical values. Instead of specifying the rule\n‘This is what a cat looks like’ in thousands of sentences, we allow a\nmodel to learn statistical features from suitable example images.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Examples + learning objective\n         │\n         ▼\n   Learning method\n         │\n         ▼\nAdjusted model parameters\n         │\n         ▼\nNew input -&gt; prediction<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Problem this approach solves:<\/strong> Patterns can be\ncaptured for which nobody can specify a complete list of comprehensible\nrules.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> A learned statistical pattern is not\nautomatically correct, causally explained or reliable outside the\ntraining distribution. Quality depends, among other things, on the data,\nlearning method, model and evaluation.<\/p>\n<p>Both approaches remain relevant today. In a document platform, a\nlearned model can suggest topics while explicit rules continue to\nenforce user permissions and deletion deadlines.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>1958 – The perceptron and the hope of learning machines<\/h2>\n<p>In 1958, Frank Rosenblatt presented the <strong>perceptron<\/strong>\nas a model of information processing capable of learning. A simple\nvariant combines input values with weights, sums their contributions and\ndetermines the output using a threshold. During learning, the weights\ncan be adjusted.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Feature 1 --(weight 1)--┐\nFeature 2 --(weight 2)--┼--&gt; Sum + threshold --&gt; decision\nFeature 3 --(weight 3)--┘\n                         ▲\n                     Weights are adjusted\n                     using examples<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This revealed a fundamental difference from pure rule programming:\nthe relevant numbers are not solely specified by the programmer, but can\nresult from training.<\/p>\n<p>A simple perceptron, however, can represent only certain decision\npatterns, in particular <strong>linearly separable<\/strong> classes. A\nwell-known problem is that a single-layer linear model cannot represent\nthe <strong>exclusive OR operation (XOR)<\/strong> correctly. This does\nnot prove that neural networks are unsuitable in principle: multilayer\nnetworks with appropriate nonlinear functions can represent more complex\npatterns.<\/p>\n<p>The practical difficulty at the time was to train such multilayer\nmodels reliably and efficiently. Criticism of limited models must not be\nconfused with a general refutation of all neural networks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>1960s to 1980s: Expert systems, hopes and AI winters<\/h2>\n<p>Early successes in tightly constrained tasks led to high\nexpectations. Programs could find logical proofs, solve individual\nproblems through search or apply specialist rules. However, the open\nworld of everyday life is not as manageable as a well-defined game rule\nor a small artificial problem space.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"71-was-war-ein-expertensystem\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What was an expert system?<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Expert systems<\/strong> attempted to represent specialist\nknowledge explicitly. They typically consisted of a <strong>knowledge\nbase<\/strong> containing facts and rules and an <strong>inference\nengine<\/strong> that applied rules to a specific case. Well-known\nresearch areas included chemical structure analysis and medical decision\nsupport.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User input\n      │\n      ▼\nInference engine &lt;----&gt; Knowledge base (facts and rules)\n      │\n      ▼\nResult or recommendation<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This architecture can be very effective as long as the domain is\nclearly bounded and the rules can be maintained.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operational problem:<\/strong> Knowledge must be captured,\ncontinually checked and updated. Changes in professional practice,\ntechnology or regulations may affect many rules. Moreover, the system\nknows only what is represented in its knowledge base and inference\nlogic.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"72-warum-spricht-man-von-ki-wintern\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why do people speak of ‘AI\nwinters’?<\/h3>\n<p><strong>AI winters<\/strong> are periods in which interest, funding\nand commercial confidence in certain AI approaches declined. The\n<strong>1970s<\/strong> and the <strong>late 1980s/early 1990s<\/strong>\nare often cited. The precise boundaries depend on the country, industry\nand branch of research.<\/p>\n<p>One cause was the gap between predictions and achievable performance:\ncomputers were slow, memory was limited, data was difficult to obtain,\nand many apparently simple problems turned out to be very difficult.\nLarge sets of rules were also expensive to create and maintain.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Historical caution:<\/strong> ‘Winter’ does not mean that all\nAI research worldwide stopped or that no further progress occurred. Many\nideas that became important later originated or matured precisely in\nperiods that received less attention.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"73-eine-lehre-aus-den-fruehen-rueckschlaegen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A lesson from the\nearly setbacks<\/h3>\n<p>A convincing demonstration does not yet imply a reliable, economical\napplication. We must investigate whether a method can cope with new\ndata, frequent changes, real failure cases and reasonable operating\ncosts. This insight remains relevant to present-day AI systems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>1986 – Backpropagation makes multilayer networks easier to\ntrain<\/h2>\n<p>In 1986, David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton and Ronald Williams\npublished the influential paper <em>Learning Representations by\nBack-Propagating Errors<\/em>. They described a method in which the\ndifference between the desired and computed results is used to adjust\nthe weights of a multilayer network in a targeted manner.<\/p>\n<p>In simplified terms, the process is:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Training example\n       │\n       ▼\nNetwork computes an output\n       │\n       ▼\nComparison with desired result\n       │\n       ▼\nError information is propagated backwards\n       │\n       ▼\nWeights are adjusted\n       │\n       └──────── next training round ───────────┐<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The English term <strong>backpropagation<\/strong> roughly means\n<em>propagation of the error backwards<\/em>. More precisely, derivative\ninformation is computed backwards through the network; an optimisation\nmethod can then change the weights.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important historical distinction:<\/strong> The underlying\nmathematical ideas and related methods go further back. The 1986 paper\nwas a particularly influential step towards practical adoption,\n<strong>not the first invention of every form of gradient calculation or\nautomatic differentiation<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Backpropagation alone did not produce present-day language models.\nVery large suitable datasets, powerful computers and additional\narchitectural ideas were still missing. But a key obstacle had become\nsmaller: a network could learn to adjust its internal intermediate\nfeatures, rather than modifying only the final decision layer.<\/p>\n<p>M5-L2 will explain the method using a clear numerical\ncalculation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From specialist programs to statistically learning\nsystems<\/h2>\n<p>From the 1990s onwards, statistical learning methods took on an\nincreasingly significant role in practical applications. Automatic\nclassification, language processing, search and pattern recognition\nbenefited from more data and growing computing power.<\/p>\n<p>This did not mean that every program used neural networks. Many\nclassical methods, such as decision trees or other statistical models,\nremain useful, particularly for manageable datasets. Heuristic search\nand explicitly programmed rules also remained important.<\/p>\n<p>A historically notable example is <strong>IBM Deep Blue<\/strong>,\nwhich defeated the reigning world chess champion Garry Kasparov in a\nmatch in 1997. Deep Blue used highly specialised search, evaluation\nmethods, computing power and chess knowledge. <strong>It was not a\nmodern large language model.<\/strong> Its success illustrates that\nsophisticated behaviour can arise from very different architectures.<\/p>\n<p>A second development was the recognition of data as an important\nresource. For some tasks, better and more diverse training data can\nincrease progress more than additional hand-written rules. But data\nalone is not enough: it can be biased, incomplete, erroneous or legally\nproblematic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why 2012 is regarded as a turning point in deep\nlearning<\/h2>\n<p>Multilayer neural networks existed long before 2012. However,\ntraining large models was demanding, and the available data and hardware\nlimited practical performance.<\/p>\n<p>With <strong>AlexNet<\/strong>, the image classification work of Alex\nKrizhevsky, Ilya Sutskever and Geoffrey Hinton from\n<strong>2012<\/strong>, it became clear how effective deep convolutional\nnetworks could be on large image datasets. The work combined a suitable\narchitecture, large labelled training datasets, improvements in training\nand the parallel computing power of <strong>graphics processing units\n(GPUs)<\/strong>. It achieved a striking result in ImageNet image\nclassification.<\/p>\n<p>This did not suddenly invent ‘general intelligence’. The performance\ninitially concerned a specific visual task. Its significance lay in the\ndemonstrated practical effectiveness of an entire technical direction\nand its potential for further tasks.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Why GPUs in particular?<\/strong> Many network operations\nconsist of large numbers of similar numerical calculations. GPUs can\ncarry out such calculations in parallel. This considerably accelerated\ntraining and later also the execution of many models. Performance and\ncosts, however, still depend on architecture, data transfer, memory\nsize, energy and utilisation.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Large training datasets\n         +\nSuitable network architecture\n         +\nTraining method\n         +\nParallel hardware (GPU)\n         │\n         ▼\nMore powerful deep networks in practice<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>These components reinforced one another. It would therefore be wrong\nto attribute the success solely to a graphics card or solely to a\nlearning algorithm.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>2017 – The Transformer architecture<\/h2>\n<p>Language processing had long been characterised by rule-based\nmethods, statistical models and later <strong>recurrent neural\nnetworks<\/strong>. Recurrent networks process sequences in a form in\nwhich earlier processing steps influence subsequent ones. For long\ntexts, learning across widely separated positions can be difficult, and\nprocessing can be hard to parallelise.<\/p>\n<p>In 2017, Ashish Vaswani and co-authors published <em>Attention Is All\nYou Need<\/em>. The proposed <strong>Transformer<\/strong> used attention\nmechanisms as a central way of processing relationships between parts of\na sequence without relying, in its original architecture, on the\npreviously widespread recurrent sequence processing.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Attention<\/strong> can be understood as <em>attention\nweighting<\/em>: when processing one position, other relevant positions\nare taken into account to different degrees. In modern models, this is a\nmathematical operation on numerical vectors, not human consciousness or\nconscious attention.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Text -&gt; segments (tokens) -&gt; numerical representations\n                                    │\n                                    ▼\n                            Transformer layers\n                      Relationships between positions\n                                    │\n                                    ▼\n                           Further numerical output<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The architecture facilitated certain forms of parallel training and\nformed an important foundation for numerous later language and\nmultimodal models. It was developed beyond its original application in\nmachine translation.<\/p>\n<p>One distinction is essential: <strong>not every Transformer is a\nchatbot<\/strong>, and not every Transformer model predicts only the next\nword. There are, among others, encoder, decoder and encoder-decoder\nvariants. These differences are explained in detail in M6-L1.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>2020s – Large language models and conversational\nassistants<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>large language model (LLM)<\/strong> processes language-like\nsequences of symbols, usually <strong>tokens<\/strong>. Tokens are units\nof a segmentation used by the model, such as parts of words, entire\nwords or sequences of punctuation marks. In many generative LLMs, a\ncentral training task is to predict the next token from previous tokens.\nThrough very large numbers of such predictions and parameter\nadjustments, models emerge that can use complex linguistic patterns.<\/p>\n<p>The research field developed in several stages: extensive\n<strong>pretraining<\/strong> on large datasets produced versatile base\nmodels. Methods of adapting them to instructions and human preferences\nmade them more useful for conversations and assistance. An important\nmilestone was research into <strong>few-shot learning<\/strong>: a model\ncan often recognise the intended task from a few examples in the input\ntext without its weights having to be changed each time.<\/p>\n<p>Public perception thus shifted from individual specialist\napplications to generalisable user interfaces: people could talk to one\nsystem about very different tasks rather than opening a separate tool\nfor each task.<\/p>\n<p>But the impression of a single intelligent machine obscures several\ntechnical layers:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User question\n    │\n    ▼\nApplication: conversation management, rules, possible tool access\n    │\n    ▼\nLLM: context processing and token prediction\n    │\n    ▼\nOutput text\n    │\n    └── if needed: verification, sources, database or external tools<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The model has learned parameters; a chat application may additionally\nuse conversation context, retrievable documents, search functions or\nactual program actions. <strong>An LLM is therefore not identical to the\nentire assistance software.<\/strong> Nor does generating a plausible\nsentence guarantee that the statement is correct.<\/p>\n<p>Terms such as ‘knowledge’, ‘understanding’, ‘reasoning’ and\n‘consciousness’ are operationalised differently in AI and are partly\nsubject to scientific or philosophical debate. For practical technical\nassessment, it is initially more important to establish which task a\nsystem demonstrably performs reliably under which conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Development at a glance<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Date<\/th>\n<th>Milestone<\/th>\n<th>What changed technically<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1943<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>McCulloch and Pitts<\/td>\n<td>Neuron-like logical computational units formalised<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1950<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Turing<\/td>\n<td>Machine language behaviour discussed as a testable research\nproblem<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1955/1956<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Dartmouth proposal and workshop<\/td>\n<td>AI made visible as a distinct research programme<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1958<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Rosenblatt and the perceptron<\/td>\n<td>Learning weighted connections as a concrete model idea<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1960s–1980s<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Symbolic systems and expert systems<\/td>\n<td>Explicit rules and knowledge bases for specialist tasks<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1970s, late 1980s<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>AI winters<\/td>\n<td>Limits of ambitious promises and high development costs became\napparent<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1986<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Influential backpropagation paper<\/td>\n<td>More targeted training of multilayer networks; earlier foundations\ndeveloped further<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1997<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Deep Blue<\/td>\n<td>Specialised search and substantial computing power at the highest\nlevel in chess<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2012<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>AlexNet<\/td>\n<td>Powerful deep image networks using large datasets and GPUs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2017<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Transformer<\/td>\n<td>Effective attention architecture for sequence processing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2020s<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Large-scale language models and assistants<\/td>\n<td>Versatile text processing, generative models and additional\ntools<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_030_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Historical timeline of selected AI developments\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Selected AI milestones from the 1956 Dartmouth\nmeeting through backpropagation, deep learning and transformers to LLM\nassistants.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><strong>Note:<\/strong> This table provides orientation. Many\ndevelopments occurred simultaneously, and the dates are not exclusive\ninvention dates for all the respective methods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What modern AI systems can actually do – and where\nlimitations remain<\/h2>\n<p>Under suitable conditions, modern models can write, translate and\nsummarise texts, structure information from documents, recognise\npatterns in images, explain programs or generate new media content. With\nsuitable software they can also use tools, for example searching a\ndatabase, calling a script or supporting a multistep process.<\/p>\n<p>Limitations remain relevant:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Factual reliability:<\/strong> A convincingly worded text may\nbe factually wrong. For current, legal, medical or financial questions,\nsource and plausibility checks are necessary.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Training and the present:<\/strong> A model can work only\nwith what its weights, current context and any connected information\nsources allow. Fluency does not replace access to live data.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Robustness:<\/strong> Small changes in inputs, unusual cases\nor targeted manipulation can degrade results.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Data protection and security:<\/strong> Uploaded customer\ndata, API keys or personal documents require appropriate access and\nprocessing controls.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Costs and operation:<\/strong> Large models require computing\npower and memory; even locally operated models consume energy and\nrequire maintenance of their software environment.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Responsibility:<\/strong> When AI prepares a decision or\ntriggers an action, it must be clear who controls it, verifies it and\ntakes responsibility if something goes wrong.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Especially in robotics<\/strong>, the boundary between\nlinguistic advice and immediate physical action is important. A language\nmodel can suggest a work plan, but safety-critical motor control also\nrequires reliable control systems, sensor checks, limits and emergency\nshutdown. We discuss this in M6-L7.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three application examples: From the old principle to the\nmodern solution<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"151-lokaler-medienkatalog\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local media catalogue<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Traditional approach:<\/strong> Files and metadata are\norganised using fixed filename patterns, tags and database queries. With\nclear rules, this is fast, inexpensive and controllable.<\/p>\n<p><strong>With AI:<\/strong> A model suggests topics or descriptions for\npreviously unstructured media. The results are stored as suggestions,\nnot infallible facts. User permissions, file access and final approvals\nremain deterministic software functions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> If all files are already\nreliably named and categorised, a large neural network would merely add\noverhead.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"152-online-analysewerkzeug\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Online analysis tool<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Traditional approach:<\/strong> Calculations, format checks\nand database operations follow fixed implemented rules. The results are\nreproducible.<\/p>\n<p><strong>With AI:<\/strong> Natural-language input can translate an\nanalysis request into structured parameters or produce a report. The\nactual numerical calculations continue to be performed by verifiable\nsoftware.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Risk:<\/strong> Without clear separation, a model could invent\nnumbers or suggest unauthorised data access. The software must check\naccess permissions and input validation independently of the model.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"153-ki-auftraege-in-einer-serverarchitektur\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>AI jobs in a server\narchitecture<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Traditional approach:<\/strong> A web service accepts jobs,\nstores them in a database and distributes them to workers through a\nqueue.<\/p>\n<p><strong>With AI:<\/strong> A worker loads a trained model, processes\nthe input and stores a result. The remaining infrastructure monitors\njobs, limits resources and provides retry and billing logic.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User -&gt; Web application -&gt; Job database / queue\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n                           Worker with AI model\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n                             Result + status\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n                                 User<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This illustration connects the AI section with B (servers), C\n(databases) and D (cloud operations). The AI model is <strong>one\ncomponent within an operational software system<\/strong>, not\nautomatically its entire architecture.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When is each approach appropriate?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Task<\/th>\n<th>Often the natural approach<\/th>\n<th>Why not automatically a large LLM?<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Convert a fixed filename<\/td>\n<td>Clearly defined rules<\/td>\n<td>Fast, understandable, without model costs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Calculate an invoice total<\/td>\n<td>Deterministic algorithm<\/td>\n<td>Exact arithmetic should not depend on text probabilities<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sort free text by topic<\/td>\n<td>Classical ML or language model<\/td>\n<td>Choice depends on data volume, quality and costs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Describe the content of an image<\/td>\n<td>Suitable image/multimodal model<\/td>\n<td>Need for perceptual patterns rather than just rules<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Search documents<\/td>\n<td>Search index, possibly supplemented by AI<\/td>\n<td>Sources must remain retrievable and verifiable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A robot navigates<\/td>\n<td>Sensors, localisation, control, planning<\/td>\n<td>Language generation does not replace safe motion control<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The historical perspective makes clear that different AI approaches\nsolve different problems. An AI system does not become better simply by\nincluding as many modern components as possible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M5-L2<\/h2>\n<p>The history of AI can be read as a recurring movement between three\nquestions:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>How can knowledge and inference rules be described\nexplicitly?<\/strong> This led to symbolic AI, search methods and expert\nsystems.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>How can programs learn rules from examples?<\/strong> From\nthis developed statistical learning methods, perceptrons and multilayer\nneural networks.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>How can complex models be trained and operated in practice\nusing large datasets?<\/strong> New learning methods, suitable\narchitectural ideas, GPUs, data infrastructure and distributed systems\ncontributed to this.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Present-day language models stand at the end of this line of\ndevelopment, not outside it. They are powerful, statistically trained\ncomponents. Their practical usefulness often arises only through an\nappropriate application, verified data, tools, safety rules and\noperational concepts.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson: <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2 –\nWhat is a neural network?<\/a><\/strong> There we examine the artificial\nneuron, weights, activation functions and layers using a numerical\ncalculation that can be followed step by step. We also explain how a\nnetwork can learn from errors and why more layers do not automatically\nproduce better results.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 4 November 2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Subject-specific source review: 4 November 2026.<\/strong> The\nfocus is on original historical papers and established research\narchives. The teaching examples, architectural sketches and comparisons\nare original simplified representations, not quotations from the\nsources. No claims are made about current product features or provider\nprices.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>McCulloch, W. S.; Pitts, W. (1943):<\/strong> <em>A Logical\nCalculus of the Ideas Immanent in Nervous Activity<\/em>. <em>Bulletin of\nMathematical Biophysics<\/em>. <a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02478259\">https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02478259<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Turing, A. M. (1950):<\/strong> <em>Computing Machinery and\nIntelligence<\/em>. <em>Mind<\/em>, 59(236), 433–460. <a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.1093/mind/LIX.236.433\">https://doi.org/10.1093/mind/LIX.236.433<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>McCarthy, J.; Minsky, M.; Rochester, N.; Shannon, C. (1955;\nreprint 2006):<\/strong> <em>A Proposal for the Dartmouth Summer Research\nProject on Artificial Intelligence<\/em>. <a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.1609/aimag.v27i4.1904\">https://doi.org/10.1609/aimag.v27i4.1904<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Rosenblatt, F. (1958):<\/strong> <em>The Perceptron: A\nProbabilistic Model for Information Storage and Organization in the\nBrain<\/em>. <em>Psychological Review<\/em>, 65(6), 386–408. <a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.1037/h0042519\">https://doi.org/10.1037/h0042519<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Stanford AI100:<\/strong> Timeline of AI development,\nincluding Dartmouth, DENDRAL and Deep Blue. <a\nhref=\"https://ai100.stanford.edu/about/history\">https://ai100.stanford.edu/about/history<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>KU Leuven:<\/strong> Overview of AI history, expert systems\nand AI winters (additional context). <a\nhref=\"https://ai.kuleuven.be/aivooriedereen/en/history\">https://ai.kuleuven.be/aivooriedereen/en/history<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Rumelhart, D. E.; Hinton, G. E.; Williams, R. J.\n(1986):<\/strong> <em>Learning Representations by Back-Propagating\nErrors<\/em>. <em>Nature<\/em>, 323, 533–536. <a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.1038/323533a0\">https://doi.org/10.1038/323533a0<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>IBM:<\/strong> Historical account and technical background of\nDeep Blue (1997). <a\nhref=\"https://www.ibm.com/history/deep-blue\">https://www.ibm.com/history/deep-blue<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">9.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Krizhevsky, A.; Sutskever, I.; Hinton, G. (2012):<\/strong>\n<em>ImageNet Classification with Deep Convolutional Neural\nNetworks<\/em>. <em>NeurIPS 2012<\/em>. <a\nhref=\"https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2012/hash/c399862d3b9d6b76c8436e924a68c45b-Abstract.html\">https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2012/hash/c399862d3b9d6b76c8436e924a68c45b-Abstract.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">10.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Vaswani, A. et al. (2017):<\/strong> <em>Attention Is All You\nNeed<\/em>. <a\nhref=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762\">https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">11.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Brown, T. B. et al. (2020):<\/strong> <em>Language Models are\nFew-Shot Learners<\/em>. <a\nhref=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165\">https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">12.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Ouyang, L. et al. (2022):<\/strong> <em>Training Language\nModels to Follow Instructions with Human Feedback<\/em>. <a\nhref=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155\">https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> The history of research cannot be reduced\nto twelve papers. These sources are verifiable milestones, not a claim\nto cover all precursors, alternatives or contributors in full.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M5-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M5-L2 – What is a neural network?<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor’s: AI fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Initial question: How can a machine discover a rule from\nexamples?<\/h2>\n<p>A conventional program processes data according to explicitly\nformulated instructions. A photo archive might, for instance, determine\nwhether a file belongs to a particular category using its filename or a\ntag assigned by the user. But what fixed rule could recognise a cat in a\npreviously unseen photograph? Fur colour, head position, lighting and\nframing vary constantly. An endless list of special cases is of little\nhelp.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>artificial neural network<\/strong> is a mathematical model\nwith many adjustable numbers that are adapted using example data. It\nreceives inputs, calculates an output from them and compares that output\nwith a learning objective during training. A learning procedure then\nchanges its internal numbers so that the output fits the training\nexamples better where possible. Ideally, it also recognises relevant\npatterns in unseen data. This is known as\n<strong>generalisation<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>The essential point is that a neural network does <em>not become\nintelligent by itself<\/em> simply because it uses artificial neurons. It\ncomputes a function with a very large number of parameters. The task\nperformed by that function depends on the architecture, data, training\nobjective and application. Image recognition, language processing and\nprediction use similar building blocks but do not necessarily use the\nsame overall architecture.<\/p>\n<p>This lesson makes the internal computations visible. We first examine\na single artificial computational unit, then two layers, and finally\n<strong>one fully worked learning step<\/strong>. The later lessons\nM5-L3–M6-L3 explain in more detail how the resulting systems are trained\nand developed into language models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>‘Neuron’ is a metaphor, not a miniature brain cell<\/h2>\n<p>The historical work of McCulloch and Pitts (1943), and later of\nRosenblatt and Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams, provides important\nstarting points (see M5-L1). Nevertheless, a modern artificial neuron is\nnot a detailed computer simulation of a biological nerve cell.\nBiological neurons are electrochemical, time-dependent cells with\nfeedback connections. The computational unit discussed here essentially\nconsists of <strong>multiplications, additions and a mathematical\nactivation function<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>The analogy is limited: multiple inputs influence an outcome, and the\nstrength of their influence can be changed. This is sufficient for a\nuseful computational model. It does not support conclusions about human\nconsciousness, feelings or thought processes.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Biological inspiration:             Mathematical implementation:\n\nmultiple input signals               numbers x1, x2, ...\n         │                                    │\n         ▼                                    ▼\nprocessing in a cell                 weighted sum + bias\n         │                                    │\n         ▼                                    ▼\ntransmitted signal                   activation function\n                                              │\n                                              ▼\n                                         numerical output<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The modern term <strong>Deep Learning<\/strong> refers primarily to\ntraining models with multiple processing layers. ‘Deep’ is a property of\nthe mathematical architecture, not a claim about depth in the\nphilosophical sense.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The smallest calculation: Inputs, weights and bias<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose that a computational unit is to determine a number from two\nmeasurements. The inputs are called <code>x1<\/code> and <code>x2<\/code>.\nEach has a <strong>weight<\/strong> (<code>w1<\/code>, <code>w2<\/code>)\nthat determines its influence on the intermediate result. There is also\na <strong>bias<\/strong> (<code>b<\/code>), an adjustable base value or\noffset.<\/p>\n<p>The basic formula is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=x_1\\times w_1+x_2\\times w_2+b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For two inputs <code>x1 = 2<\/code> and <code>x2 = 3<\/code>, weights\n<code>w1 = 0,5<\/code>, <code>w2 = −0,2<\/code> and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = 0{,}1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe calculation gives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>z<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nz&amp;=2\\times0{,}5+3\\times(-0{,}2)+0{,}1\\\\\n &amp;=1{,}0-0{,}6+0{,}1\\\\\n &amp;=0{,}5\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The <strong>inputs<\/strong> come from the data to be processed. The\n<strong>weights and bias values<\/strong> belong to the model and are\nchanged during training. A weight can be positive or negative: for an\ninput with the same sign, a positive weight increases the sum and a\nnegative one decreases it. The bias allows the behaviour to be shifted\nwithout changing the input data.<\/p>\n<p>Is this already learning? No. So far, only a calculation rule has\nbeen defined. It becomes a training procedure only when the parameters\nare deliberately changed on the basis of an error.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"31-was-ist-ein-merkmal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is a feature?<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>feature<\/strong> is a numerical value received by the model\nas input. For a simple estimate, these could be a temperature and a time\nof day. In images, they might be pixel values; in more complex networks,\ninternal representations are also passed on as features.<\/p>\n<p>Real input data must be prepared appropriately. A temperature of\n<code>20<\/code> and a turnover of <code>2.000.000<\/code> have very\ndifferent orders of magnitude. Without appropriate <strong>scaling or\nnormalisation<\/strong>, some features can make optimisation\nunnecessarily difficult. Preprocessing therefore belongs to the model\narchitecture, even though it is not itself a neuron.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why an activation function is necessary<\/h2>\n<p>The weighted sum <code>z<\/code> is often followed by an\n<strong>activation function<\/strong>. It maps the number to a new value.\nA common example is the <strong>Rectified Linear Unit\n(ReLU)<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>⇒<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>⇒<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>⇒<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{ReLU}(z)&amp;=\\max(0,z)\\\\\nz=1{,}5&amp;\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\operatorname{ReLU}(z)=1{,}5\\\\\nz=-0{,}8&amp;\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\operatorname{ReLU}(z)=0\\\\\nz=0&amp;\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\operatorname{ReLU}(z)=0\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>ReLU lets positive values pass and sets negative ones to zero. Other\nfunctions include <strong>sigmoid<\/strong> (which returns a value\nbetween 0 and 1) and <code>tanh<\/code> (which returns a value between −1\nand 1). The appropriate function depends on the task, layer and training\nprocedure. Not every type of output neuron needs the same activation\nfunction as a hidden layer.<\/p>\n<p>Why is a long chain of weighted sums not sufficient? Because several\nconsecutive linear or affine calculations without an intervening\nnonlinearity can be combined into <strong>a single calculation of that\nkind<\/strong>. More layers would then provide no fundamentally greater\nexpressive power for nonlinear relationships. The activation removes\nthis restriction.<\/p>\n<p>A famous thought experiment is the <strong>XOR operation<\/strong>\n(<em>Exclusive Or<\/em>): the output is 1 precisely when exactly one of\ntwo binary inputs is 1. A single linear decision cannot correctly\nseparate the four cases. Several suitable computational units and\nnonlinear activations can represent them, however. This does not mean\nthat a network reliably <em>learns<\/em> any desired rule from\narbitrarily little data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From neurons to layers and a network<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>layer<\/strong> contains several such computational units\nthat calculate new numbers from previous results. Typically, a\ndistinction is made between an input layer, one or more <strong>hidden\nlayers<\/strong> and an output layer.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_031_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Feedforward network with inputs, hidden layer and output\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: This example feedforward network connects three\ninput nodes to four hidden neurons and two output nodes; its edges carry\ntrainable weights.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Input data                   hidden layer                       output\n\n x1 ───┬─────────────────&gt; [h1: Sum + ReLU] ─────┐\n       │                                        ├──&gt; [Output neuron] → y_predicted\n       └─────┐                                  │\n             │                                  │\n x2 ─────────┴───────────&gt; [h2: Sum + ReLU] ─────┘\n\n   two features                 two neurons                   one numerical value<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>In this sketch, each of the two hidden neurons receives <em>both<\/em>\ninput values; for clarity, not all connections are drawn separately.\nThis is called a <strong>fully connected layer<\/strong> (<em>dense\nlayer<\/em>). The internal layer does not necessarily correspond to a\nconcept that humans can name, such as ‘cat’s ear’ or ‘sentence meaning’.\nWhich intermediate patterns it actually represents is an empirical\nquestion and often difficult to interpret.<\/p>\n<p>The computation from left to right is called the <strong>forward\npass<\/strong>. It is the normal computational path for a prediction and\nalso the first part of every training step.<\/p>\n<p>How many adjustable numbers does our mini-network have? Each of the\ntwo hidden neurons has two weights and a bias:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2 \\times  3 = 6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe output requires two more weights and a bias: <code>3<\/code>. This\ngives a total of <strong>nine trainable parameters<\/strong>. The input\nvalues themselves are not trainable parameters.<\/p>\n<p>In large models, the parameter count increases sharply. More\nparameters often increase representational capacity, but also memory\nrequirements, computational cost and the risk of overfitting. The number\nalone says nothing reliable about the quality or understanding of a\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The complete numerical example – the first prediction<\/h2>\n<p>We now work through <em>this particular network<\/em> in full. All the\nnumbers are invented for teaching purposes; they do not come from a real\ntrained model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Given:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Inputs: <code>x1 = 1<\/code>, <code>x2 = 2<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Neuron <code>h1<\/code>: weights <code>0,5<\/code> and\n<code>0,25<\/code>, bias <code>−0,5<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Neuron <code>h2<\/code>: weights <code>−0,4<\/code> and\n<code>0,6<\/code>, bias <code>0<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both hidden neurons use ReLU.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Output neuron: weights <code>0,8<\/code> for <code>h1<\/code> and\n<code>−0,25<\/code> for <code>h2<\/code>, bias <code>0,1<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The output neuron uses <strong>no additional activation<\/strong>: it\nreturns an arbitrary real number. Our exercise is a very simple\nnumerical prediction, not a probability estimate.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The desired <strong>target value<\/strong> for this single example is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y = 1{,}0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"61-erste-verborgene-einheit\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>First hidden unit<\/h3>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nz_1&amp;=1\\times0{,}5+2\\times0{,}25-0{,}5\\\\\n   &amp;=0{,}5+0{,}5-0{,}5\\\\\n   &amp;=0{,}5\\\\\nh_1&amp;=\\operatorname{ReLU}(0{,}5)=0{,}5\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h3 id=\"62-zweite-verborgene-einheit\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Second hidden unit<\/h3>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nz_2&amp;=1\\times(-0{,}4)+2\\times0{,}6+0\\\\\n   &amp;=-0{,}4+1{,}2\\\\\n   &amp;=0{,}8\\\\\nh_2&amp;=\\operatorname{ReLU}(0{,}8)=0{,}8\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h3 id=\"63-ausgabeneuron\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Output neuron<\/h3>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\ny_{\\mathrm{predicted}}&amp;=h_1\\times0{,}8+h_2\\times(-0{,}25)+0{,}1\\\\\n &amp;=0{,}5\\times0{,}8+0{,}8\\times(-0{,}25)+0{,}1\\\\\n &amp;=0{,}4-0{,}2+0{,}1\\\\\n &amp;=0{,}3\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With its current nine parameters, the network has therefore\ncalculated the prediction <strong><code>0,3<\/code><\/strong> for the\ninputs <code>(1, 2)<\/code>. However, the target value is\n<strong><code>1,0<\/code><\/strong>. The model is thus <code>0,7<\/code>\ntoo low.<\/p>\n<p>Anyone who has followed this far already knows the essential\ncalculation involved in making a prediction: <em>each unit computes a\nweighted sum, applies an activation where appropriate and passes the\nresult to the next layer.<\/em> A large network repeats this principle in\na more elaborate form and with many specialised building blocks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Making an error measurable: The loss function<\/h2>\n<p>During training, it is not enough to know <em>that<\/em> the\nprediction is wrong. An optimisation procedure needs a number expressing\nhow far the result is from the objective. This is the <strong>loss\nfunction<\/strong>. For mathematical reasons, we choose half the squared\ndeviation for our numerical prediction:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Prediction<\/mtext><mo>−<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Target value<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>49<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>245<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{Loss}&amp;=\\frac12\\times{(\\text{Prediction}-\\text{Target value})}^2\\\\\n &amp;=\\frac12\\times{(0{,}3-1{,}0)}^2\\\\\n &amp;=\\frac12\\times{(-0{,}7)}^2\\\\\n &amp;=\\frac12\\times0{,}49\\\\\n &amp;=0{,}245\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The value <strong><code>0,245<\/code><\/strong> describes only the\nerror for our single example and our freely chosen loss function. It is\nnot a general measure of neural network quality. Other tasks require\nother loss functions: classification often uses variants of\n<strong>cross-entropy<\/strong>, while numerical predictions often use\nsquared or absolute errors.<\/p>\n<p>A model with low training loss is not yet a good product. It might\nhave memorised or fitted the training examples too closely and might\nfail on new data. Separate evaluation datasets are therefore\nindispensable; more on this later.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The crucial question: Which number must we change?<\/h2>\n<p>Our nine parameters influence the result in different ways. We could\nblindly change an arbitrary weight. However, that would be extremely\ninefficient, especially with millions of parameters. Instead, we\ncalculate for every parameter <strong>how a small change affects the\nerror<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Such a local rate of change is called a <strong>derivative<\/strong>.\nAll the derivatives of the loss function with respect to the parameters\ntogether form the <strong>gradient<\/strong>. The loss function can be\nimagined as a landscape of elevations: the parameters determine where we\nstand; the gradient points locally in the direction of steepest ascent.\nTo reduce the error, we move in the opposite direction.<\/p>\n<p>This basic procedure is called <strong>gradient descent<\/strong>. The\nsize of the step is determined by the <strong>learning rate<\/strong>. A\nhigh learning rate can lead to unstable jumps; a rate that is too low\ncan greatly slow down training. The gradient tells us only about the\nlocal neighbourhood. It does <strong>not<\/strong> guarantee that we will\nfind the global optimum or that every individual step will reduce the\nloss.<\/p>\n<p>To determine the derivatives efficiently in a multilayer network, we\nuse <strong>backpropagation<\/strong>: passing derivative information\nbackwards through the already known computations. The mathematical tool\nis the <strong>chain rule<\/strong>. ‘Backwards’ refers to the\n<em>calculation of the gradients<\/em>; the input data or information do\nnot run backwards as in a film.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Backpropagation in full, without missing calculations<\/h2>\n<p>We use exactly the parameters and intermediate values from Section 6.\nThe loss function is still\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\frac12\\times{(\\hat y-y)}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt the output, the derivative of the loss with respect to the prediction\nis:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}\\hat y}=\\hat y-y=0{,}3-1{,}0=-0{,}7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The symbol <code>dL/dŷ<\/code> means: how does the loss change when\nthe prediction changes slightly? The minus sign here indicates that a\nlarger prediction would initially reduce the error.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"91-die-drei-parameter-der-ausgabeschicht\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The three parameters\nof the output layer<\/h3>\n<p>The output is calculated from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=0{,}8\\times h_1-0{,}25\\times h_2+0{,}1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nApplying the chain rule yields:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Derivative with respect to output weight 1:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>35<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Derivative with respect to output weight 2:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>56<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Derivative with respect to output bias:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Derivative with respect to output weight 1:}\\quad\n \\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}w_{\\mathrm{out1}}}\n &amp;=(-0{,}7)\\times h_1=(-0{,}7)\\times0{,}5=-0{,}35\\\\\n\\text{Derivative with respect to output weight 2:}\\quad\n \\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}w_{\\mathrm{out2}}}\n &amp;=(-0{,}7)\\times h_2=(-0{,}7)\\times0{,}8=-0{,}56\\\\\n\\text{Derivative with respect to output bias:}\\quad\n \\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}b_{\\mathrm{out}}}\n &amp;=-0{,}7\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A negative gradient means that if we <em>increase<\/em> the parameter\nslightly, the loss initially decreases. Gradient descent therefore\nsubtracts a negative value, making the number larger.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"92-was-passiert-mit-den-verborgenen-einheiten\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What happens to\nthe hidden units?<\/h3>\n<p>The error is now propagated backwards through the connections. For\n<code>h1<\/code>, this gives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>56<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}h_1}=(-0{,}7)\\times0{,}8=-0{,}56<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For <code>h2<\/code>, the negative output weight gives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>175<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}h_2}=(-0{,}7)\\times(-0{,}25)=+0{,}175<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The activation of both hidden neurons was positive (<code>0,5<\/code>\nand <code>0,8<\/code>). The derivative of ReLU in the positive range is\n<strong>1<\/strong>. The information can therefore be propagated\nbackwards here without any additional change. If the respective\npreactivation were negative, the ReLU derivative would be\n<strong>0<\/strong>, and no gradient would return through that unit for\nthe example considered. This very difference explains part of the\ndifficulty of training deep networks.<\/p>\n<p>For <code>h1<\/code> with inputs <code>(1, 2)<\/code>, we now\nobtain:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>56<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>56<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>56<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>56<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}w_{\\mathrm{h1x1}}}&amp;=-0{,}56\\times1=-0{,}56\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}w_{\\mathrm{h1x2}}}&amp;=-0{,}56\\times2=-1{,}12\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}b_{\\mathrm{h1}}}&amp;=-0{,}56\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For <code>h2<\/code>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>175<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>175<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>175<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>350<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>175<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}w_{\\mathrm{h2x1}}}&amp;=+0{,}175\\times1=+0{,}175\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}w_{\\mathrm{h2x2}}}&amp;=+0{,}175\\times2=+0{,}350\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}L}{\\mathrm{d}b_{\\mathrm{h2}}}&amp;=+0{,}175\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The gradients of <strong>all nine parameters<\/strong> are now known.\nWe have neither ‘guessed’ feedback nor simply transferred the sign of\nthe error to every weight. The calculation takes account of each\nparameter’s particular path to the output.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"93-eine-lernrate-neun-gleichzeitige-aenderungen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>One learning\nrate, nine simultaneous changes<\/h3>\n<p>We choose the learning rate <code>0,1<\/code>. The update rule for\neach parameter is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">new parameter<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">old parameter<\/mtext><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">its gradient<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{new parameter}=\\text{old parameter}-0{,}1\\times\\text{its gradient}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Important: all nine gradients were calculated using <strong>the same\nold parameter values<\/strong>. Only afterwards are the new values set.\nIn this example, already updated output weights must not be inserted\ninto the previously calculated gradients of the hidden layer.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Parameter<\/th>\n<th>Before<\/th>\n<th>Gradient<\/th>\n<th>After<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Weight <code>x1 → h1<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>0,5000<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,5600<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>0,5560<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weight <code>x2 → h1<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>0,2500<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−1,1200<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>0,3620<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bias <code>h1<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,5000<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,5600<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,4440<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weight <code>x1 → h2<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,4000<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>+0,1750<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,4175<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weight <code>x2 → h2<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>0,6000<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>+0,3500<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>0,5650<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bias <code>h2<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>0,0000<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>+0,1750<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,0175<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weight <code>h1 → output<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>0,8000<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,3500<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>0,8350<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weight <code>h2 → output<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,2500<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,5600<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,1940<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Output bias<\/td>\n<td><code>0,1000<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>−0,7000<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>0,1700<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This completes <strong>one entire training step<\/strong>. Real\ntraining consists of many such steps over many examples, not a single\npair of numbers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Checking: Is the prediction better now?<\/h2>\n<p>Let us insert the nine updated values, again with the\n<strong>same<\/strong> inputs <code>(1, 2)<\/code>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>556<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>362<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>444<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>836<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>836<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4175<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>565<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0175<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ReLU<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>695<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>695<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>836<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>835<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>695<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>194<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>170<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>69806<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>13483<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>170<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>73323<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nh_{1,\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=\\operatorname{ReLU}(1\\times0{,}556+2\\times0{,}362-0{,}444)\\\\\n &amp;=\\operatorname{ReLU}(0{,}836)=0{,}836\\\\\nh_{2,\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=\\operatorname{ReLU}(1\\times(-0{,}4175)+2\\times0{,}565-0{,}0175)\\\\\n &amp;=\\operatorname{ReLU}(0{,}695)=0{,}695\\\\\n\\hat y_{\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=0{,}836\\times0{,}835+0{,}695\\times(-0{,}194)+0{,}170\\\\\n &amp;=0{,}69806-0{,}13483+0{,}170\\\\\n &amp;=0{,}73323\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The new loss is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>73323<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>26677<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>03558<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nL_{\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=\\frac12\\times{(0{,}73323-1{,}0)}^2\\\\\n &amp;=\\frac12\\times{(-0{,}26677)}^2\\\\\n &amp;\\approx0{,}03558\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State<\/th>\n<th>Prediction<\/th>\n<th>Target<\/th>\n<th>Loss<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Before the step<\/td>\n<td><code>0,30000<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>1,00000<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>0,24500<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>After the step<\/td>\n<td><code>0,73323<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>1,00000<\/code><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>03558<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx  0{,}03558<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>The error for this single example has decreased.<\/strong>\nThis demonstrates the mechanism, but proves neither general learning\nability nor good predictions for unseen cases. We used one example and\nadjusted nine parameters to it; a model could easily become tailored to\nthis particular example. With an unsuitable learning rate, another\ntraining step might even increase the error. The numbers were\nindependently checked arithmetically.<\/p>\n<p>The most important result of this lesson is this: <strong>training\nmeans repeatedly and systematically changing model parameters on the\nbasis of the measured error, not a program rewriting its own rules as\ntext.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a single example to a realistic training process<\/h2>\n<p>A useful network receives not just one data point, but numerous\nexamples. A typical procedure looks like this in simplified form:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Training examples\n      │\n      ▼\nPreprocessing / feature representation\n      │\n      ▼\nForward pass: current weights produce predictions\n      │\n      ▼\nLoss: comparison with learning objectives\n      │\n      ▼\nBackpropagation: derivatives for model parameters\n      │\n      ▼\nOptimiser: update weights and bias values\n      │\n      └────────────── repeat ───────────────────┐\n                                                 │\n               Validation / tests &lt;─────────────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_032_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Training loop: mini-batch, forward pass, loss, backpropagation and optimiser\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Training loop: mini-batch, forward pass,\nloss, backpropagation and optimiser<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Simplified training cycle: a mini-batch produces\nprediction errors, which yield gradients, and an optimiser changes the\nweights for later steps. Validation data are used for model selection,\nnot direct gradient calculation; test data are held out.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In <strong>stochastic gradient descent<\/strong> (SGD), the model\nparameters are updated using small subsets of the training data. Such a\nsubset is called a <strong>mini-batch<\/strong>; the number of examples\nit contains is the <strong>batch size<\/strong>. A complete pass through\nthe intended training dataset is called an <strong>epoch<\/strong>. In\n<strong>batch gradient descent<\/strong>, by contrast, the entire\ntraining set would be used for a step. In everyday usage, these methods\nare not always strictly distinguished in terminology.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>optimiser<\/strong> is the method used to adjust parameters\non the basis of gradients. Simple gradient descent is the basic model;\nmethods such as Adam use additional information from earlier gradients.\nThese are not magical learning methods: they can facilitate\noptimisation, but they do not eliminate poor data or incorrect learning\nobjectives.<\/p>\n<p>The weights are usually <strong>initialised<\/strong> appropriately\nfirst. If they all start at the same value, symmetric neurons may learn\nthe same patterns and fail to use their additional capacity. Suitable\nrandom initialisation and the scaling of activations are therefore\npractical components of modern network architectures.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"111-parameter-und-hyperparameter\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Parameters and\nhyperparameters<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Parameters<\/strong> are the numbers changed during training,\nsuch as the nine weights and bias values in the example.\n<strong>Hyperparameters<\/strong> are settings of the training procedure\nor architecture: learning rate, number of layers, batch size or\nregularisation strength. They are not learned by the same simple\ngradient descent applied to the model weights, but are determined\nthrough selection, search or higher-level optimisation procedures.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"112-training-ist-nicht-inferenz\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Training is not inference<\/h3>\n<p>After training, the model is usually used for\n<strong>inference<\/strong>, that is, applying it to new data. An input\npasses through the learned layers and produces an output; the weights\nusually remain unchanged for that individual application. Classifying a\nphoto is inference; adapting the network using photos is training. In\ncertain systems, later adaptation or learning during operation is\npossible, but it is not automatically part of every normal\ninvocation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Generalisation: Doing more than matching training\nexamples<\/h2>\n<p>The central question is: <strong>what happens when new data look\ndifferent from previous data?<\/strong> A network can fit its training\ndata very well and nevertheless perform poorly in the outside world.\nThere are two classical categories of causes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Overfitting:<\/strong> The model also learns random\npeculiarities of the training data that do not apply to new cases. One\nexample would be a cat-image model that inadvertently uses the colour of\nthe background rather than features of the cat.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Underfitting:<\/strong> The model is too simple, inadequately\ntrained or equipped with insufficient features to capture even the basic\nrelationships in its training data. A purely linear network, for\ninstance, is fundamentally unable to solve some nonlinear tasks.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Observation<\/th>\n<th>Possible interpretation<\/th>\n<th>What to check initially<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Training and validation errors high<\/td>\n<td>Underfitting or difficult data<\/td>\n<td>Features, architecture, training, measurement errors<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Training error low, validation error much higher<\/td>\n<td>Overfitting or distribution differences<\/td>\n<td>Data splitting, leakage, model complexity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Both errors low and stable<\/td>\n<td>Encouraging, but not conclusive proof<\/td>\n<td>Representativeness, new data, edge cases<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h3 id=\"121-drei-getrennte-datenrollen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three separate data roles<\/h3>\n<p>A sound development process conceptually separates the data:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Training data<\/strong> are used to calculate gradients and\nadjust model parameters.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Validation data<\/strong> help with architecture and\nhyperparameter decisions, monitoring training and selecting among model\nversions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Test data<\/strong> are preferably used only at the end for\nan independent evaluation on previously unused examples.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>This split is not a mere formality. If the same almost identical\nphotos appear in training and testing, test performance looks too good.\nWith time-dependent data, random shuffling can leak information about\nthe future into the past. In medical data, several measurements of the\nsame person might appear in different subsets. This is generally called\n<strong>data leakage<\/strong>. How data should be split therefore\ndepends on the intended use.<\/p>\n<p>A test dataset does not guarantee the same quality permanently\neither. When the real environment changes – a different camera,\ndifferent user behaviour, a new language, new data sources – this is\ncalled <strong>distribution shift<\/strong>. Models must then be\nmonitored again and adapted if necessary.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"122-wie-ueberanpassung-reduziert-werden-kann\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How overfitting can\nbe reduced<\/h3>\n<p>Approaches include more informative and diverse data, appropriately\nsmall models, ending training early (<em>early stopping<\/em>) and\n<strong>regularisation<\/strong>. These are procedures intended to\nprevent the model from adapting its parameters arbitrarily strongly to\nindividual training cases. Examples include weight regularisation and\n<strong>dropout<\/strong>, temporarily and randomly masking certain\nactivations during training. These methods do not automatically improve\nevery model; their effectiveness must be assessed using suitable\nvalidation data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why are there different network architectures?<\/h2>\n<p>Our example is a <strong>feedforward network<\/strong>: information is\npassed from inputs to outputs in the computation shown. It is suitable\nas a basis for structured numerical values and many other tasks. Other\ntypes of data bring different requirements.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"131-convolutional-neural-networks-cnn\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Convolutional Neural\nNetworks (CNN)<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>Convolutional Neural Network<\/strong>, or CNN, often\nprocesses spatially structured data using <strong>convolutions<\/strong>.\nThe same small trainable filter weights are applied at many positions in\nan image. This is known as <strong>weight sharing<\/strong>. Compared\nwith fully connected processing of all image positions, it reduces the\nnumber of independent weights and exploits the spatial proximity of\nneighbouring pixels.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Image (pixel grid)\n      │\n      ▼\nsmall filter examines different regions in succession\n      │\n      ▼\nfeature maps\n      │\n      ▼\nfurther processing → classification / recognition<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A CNN has no built-in complete concept of objects. It learns filters\nwhose later combinations can be useful for the task. Historically, CNNs\nwere particularly important for modern image recognition; not every\ncontemporary image model uses this architecture.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"132-rekurrente-neuronale-netze-rnn\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recurrent neural networks\n(RNN)<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>Recurrent Neural Network<\/strong> (RNN) has a state that\nfeeds into the next step when processing a sequence of elements. This\nwas an important approach for text, speech and time series for a long\ntime. <strong>Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)<\/strong> is a well-known\nextension designed to mitigate certain difficulties with longer-term\ndependencies.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Word 1 → [Network state] → Word 2 → [Network state] → Word 3\n              │                          │\n              └────── information ───────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This sketch greatly simplifies the recurrent principle. For longer\nsequences, gradients can become unstable or too small; in addition, the\nstep-by-step dependency makes certain kinds of parallel computation more\ndifficult. These are among the reasons why transformers became\nparticularly important for many language tasks. However, they do not\nreplace every recurrent architecture in every application area.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"133-transformer-als-spaetere-vertiefung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transformers as a later\nadvanced topic<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>transformer<\/strong> processes relationships between input\nelements with the help of <strong>attention<\/strong>, among other\nmechanisms (a mathematical weighting of relationships). It likewise\nconsists of many trainable computational steps, including linear\ntransformations and nonlinear processing units. The principle from our\nexample – parameters, forward pass, loss, gradients and updates –\nremains the same. Only the architecture and computational paths become\nmore complex. Details follow in M6-L1.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why graphics cards often help – and where their limits\nare<\/h2>\n<p>The calculation performed by an individual neuron is simple. A large\nnetwork, however, performs a very large number of multiplications and\nadditions. Many can be grouped into <strong>matrix operations<\/strong>:\ninstead of computing each connection individually, a numerical library\nprocesses entire arrays together.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>Central Processing Unit (CPU)<\/strong>, the general-purpose\nmain processing unit, can perform such tasks. A <strong>Graphics\nProcessing Unit (GPU)<\/strong>, originally designed primarily for\ngraphics calculations, has many parallel processing units and can often\ncarry out suitable matrix operations particularly efficiently. The\nactual benefit depends on model size, memory access, parallelisability\nand hardware. A GPU is not a prerequisite for the principle of a neural\nnetwork to work.<\/p>\n<p>Available working memory and, in GPUs, <strong>video memory (VRAM,\nVideo Random Access Memory)<\/strong> limit which models and batch sizes\ncan reasonably be executed. Training typically requires extra memory for\nintermediate values, gradients and optimiser states. It can therefore be\nconsiderably more expensive than subsequent inference using the same\nmodel.<\/p>\n<p>Not all time is spent on mathematical operations: reading data,\ntransferring them between the processor and graphics memory and\ncommunicating between several computers can limit speed. A larger GPU\ncluster is therefore not automatically more economical than a single\nsuitable computer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three real-world use cases – and when a neural network is\nunnecessary<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"151-lokales-fotoarchiv\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local photo archive<\/h3>\n<p>A user wants to sort photographs by subject. A previously trained\nnetwork can translate image content into classes or features.\n<strong>Inference<\/strong> takes place on the local computer; the\nnetwork does not necessarily need internet access to sort the\nphotographs. Where the image data remain, however, depends on the\napplication actually used.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Local image folder → preprocessing → trained image model\n                                            │\n                                            ▼\n                                     features / suggestions\n                                            │\n                                            ▼\n                                     local search and correction<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Benefit:<\/strong> Search can work without manually maintained\ntags. <strong>Limitation:<\/strong> The model may confuse subjects,\nmisclassify people or rare objects, and understand data from a different\nimage domain less well. <strong>Costs:<\/strong> One-off hardware\ninvestment, electricity, storage and model updates; possibly licence\nterms of the model used. <strong>Alternative:<\/strong> For filenames,\nEXIF data and explicitly assigned tags, an ordinary database query is\noften sufficient.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"152-kleiner-online-analysedienst\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Small online analytical\nservice<\/h3>\n<p>An online service is to detect unusual measurements. A neural network\ncould learn complex patterns. However, it needs sufficiently reliable\nexamples, a suitable error measure, good tests and monitoring for false\nalarms. With manageable datasets, simple statistical thresholds or\nrobust rules can be just as useful and considerably easier to\nexplain.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer → HTTPS/API → validation and normalisation → model → result\n                                                        │\n                                                        ▼\n                                                  logging / monitoring<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Operational implications:<\/strong> Authentication, input\nretention periods, data protection, model versions, feedback on\nerroneous decisions and cost per request. Computational load and\nresponse time must match the service purchased. When a model is updated,\nearlier decisions cannot simply be assumed to remain reproducible in the\nsame way; appropriate versioning and documentation help.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"153-umfangreiche-sprach--oder-medienverarbeitung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Extensive\nlanguage or media processing<\/h3>\n<p>A more complex service combines several models: first language or\nimage analysis, then classification and finally text output if required.\nNot every stage has to consist of a large neural network. A rule-based\nchecking step, for example, can reject certain disallowed file formats\nat much lower cost.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Job → queue → preliminary check → model A → model B → result storage\n        │                            │         │\n        └── status/retry             └── measurement of errors and runtimes<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The concepts from M4-L1 (workers and queues), M4-L2 (monitoring) and\nM4-L3 (security and data protection) become relevant here. Repeated jobs\nneed unique identifiers; model errors must not be confused with\ntransport errors. <strong>Costs:<\/strong> Computing time, GPU\navailability, data transfer, storage, model operation and\ndomain-specific quality control. It is entirely possible to start with\nan external model API rather than build one’s own training\ninfrastructure; this, however, raises contractual, data protection and\ndependency issues.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations, risks and responsibility<\/h2>\n<p>A neural network represents patterns from training data and the\ntraining objective. This is both a strength and a limitation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Data quality:<\/strong> Incorrect labels, incomplete data or\none-sided examples can distort predictions. A mathematically correct\ntraining process does not guarantee a fair or technically correct\ndecision. For sensitive applications, the question of <em>which<\/em>\ndata may be used is just as important as the computational\nprocedure.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Explainability:<\/strong> Even if every individual\ncomputational step can be traced mathematically, the significance of\nmillions of interacting parameters may be difficult to interpret. ‘The\nmodel returned a high numerical value’ is not yet a sound justification\nfor a credit decision or a medical assessment.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Security:<\/strong> Input data can be deliberately\nmanipulated. Publicly accessible services additionally face abusive\nrequests and attacks designed to increase costs. Data protection and the\nability to delete data must be considered when training data are\ncollected and stored. In particular, training data must not enter freely\naccessible logs or external services without checks.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Maintenance:<\/strong> The model can lose quality as a result\nof new data or changing conditions. Suitable performance measurements,\nmodel and dataset versions, alerting and re-evaluation if necessary are\ntherefore important. Models require not only computing power during\ntraining but also a sustainable operating plan.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Cost-effectiveness:<\/strong> A neural network is not\nautomatically the best or cheapest solution. If a problem can be\nreliably solved with an SQL filter, a regular expression, a clearly\ndefined business rule or a simple statistical model, an AI component may\nonly generate higher costs, additional ways to fail and more\nmaintenance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The basic terms at a glance<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term<\/th>\n<th>Meaning in one sentence<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Neuron<\/td>\n<td>Computational unit that weights inputs and produces an output\nvalue<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weight<\/td>\n<td>Trainable influence of an input<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bias<\/td>\n<td>Trainable offset in a computational unit<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Activation function<\/td>\n<td>Often nonlinear transformation of a neuron’s result<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Layer<\/td>\n<td>Group of computational units at one processing stage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parameter<\/td>\n<td>Model number adjusted through training<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Forward pass<\/td>\n<td>Calculation from inputs to prediction<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss<\/td>\n<td>Number measuring the error according to the chosen task<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gradient<\/td>\n<td>Local derivatives of the loss with respect to the parameters<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backpropagation<\/td>\n<td>Efficient backward calculation of these derivatives<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Learning rate<\/td>\n<td>Step size of parameter updates<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inference<\/td>\n<td>Applying learned parameters to an input<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Generalisation<\/td>\n<td>Useful performance on previously unseen data<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conclusion and transition to M5-L3<\/h2>\n<p>A neural network is a <strong>composed, trainable mathematical\nfunction<\/strong>. Using inputs, weights, bias values and activation\nfunctions, it calculates an output in the forward pass. During training,\nthis is compared with a learning objective. Backpropagation calculates\nhow the parameters influence the error; an optimiser adjusts the\nnumbers. Our fully worked example shows that an error of\n<code>0,245<\/code> <em>can<\/em> fall to about <code>0,03558<\/code> after\na single learning step – without proving good performance on new\nexamples.<\/p>\n<p>The procedure explains the core of many modern AI models. But it does\nnot yet explain <strong>where suitable learning examples come from, what\nkinds of learning objectives exist or why a language model can be\ntrained without explicitly labelled sentences<\/strong>. The next lesson\nbuilds on this: <strong>M5-L3 – How machines learn<\/strong>. It covers\nsupervised, unsupervised, self-supervised and reinforcement learning, as\nwell as training data and evaluation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Technical sources checked on 04.11.2026.<\/strong> The numbers\nworked out in full here are independently chosen teaching values, not\nexternal performance figures. The following sources explain the basic\narchitecture, as well as training and generalisation procedures;\nexamples from manufacturers are not used as evidence for general\nperformance claims.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams (1986), ‘Learning\nrepresentations by back-propagating errors’.<\/strong> Original research\non backpropagation and internal representations. <em>Nature<\/em> 323,\n533–536. <a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.1038/323533a0\">https://doi.org/10.1038/323533a0<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, Machine Learning Crash Course – ‘Neural networks:\nNodes and hidden layers’.<\/strong> Simple network nodes, weights, bias,\nhidden layers and linear limitations. <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/neural-networks/nodes-hidden-layers\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/neural-networks/nodes-hidden-layers<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, Machine Learning Crash Course – ‘Neural networks:\nActivation functions’.<\/strong> Nonlinearity, sigmoid and ReLU. <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/neural-networks/activation-functions\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/neural-networks/activation-functions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, Machine Learning Crash Course – ‘Neural Networks:\nTraining using backpropagation’.<\/strong> Gradient calculation,\nvanishing/exploding gradients, ReLU and dropout. <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/neural-networks/backpropagation\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/neural-networks/backpropagation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>PyTorch, ‘Build the Neural Network’.<\/strong> Layers,\nparameter matrices, forward processing and outputs. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/buildmodel_tutorial\">https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/buildmodel_tutorial<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>PyTorch, ‘Automatic Differentiation with\ntorch.autograd’.<\/strong> Computational graphs and automatic\ndifferentiation for gradients. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/autogradqs_tutorial.html\">https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/autogradqs_tutorial.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, Machine Learning Crash Course –\n‘Overfitting’.<\/strong> Training/validation error and the limits of\ngeneralisation. <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/overfitting\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/overfitting<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>TensorFlow, ‘Overfit and underfit’.<\/strong> Underfitting,\noverfitting and regularisation. <a\nhref=\"https://www.tensorflow.org/tutorials/keras/overfit_and_underfit\">https://www.tensorflow.org/tutorials/keras/overfit_and_underfit<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The basic arithmetic in Sections 6–10 was independently checked. The\nteaching text and architecture drawings were independently produced; the\narchive contains no third-party images or training datasets.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M5-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M5-L3 – How machines learn<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor’s: AI fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A new question after M5-L2: Who tells the network what to\nlearn?<\/h2>\n<p>M5-L2 showed how a neural network processes numbers and how the error\nin a prediction feeds back into its weights. An example training step\nreduced a loss value. But this has not yet explained <strong>where the\ncorrect result comes from<\/strong> against which the network is\ncompared. Does someone have to label every single image by hand? Can a\nmodel learn something without such labels? And how does a system learn\nwhen it is supposed not only to classify images but also to choose\nactions?<\/p>\n<p>These are questions about the <strong>learning task<\/strong>, not\nsimply about the structure of the network. <em>Machine learning<\/em>, or\n<strong>ML<\/strong>, encompasses methods that obtain patterns,\nrelationships or action strategies from data and feedback instead of\nprogramming every decision rule directly. A neural network is one\npossible model family. There are also decision trees, linear models,\nnearest-neighbour methods and other techniques. Not every ML procedure\nrequires deep learning.<\/p>\n<p>The central distinction is as follows:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In <strong>supervised learning<\/strong>, a specified target result\nis known for the learning examples.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In <strong>unsupervised learning<\/strong>, we look for structures\nwithout such target labels.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In <strong>self-supervised learning<\/strong>, learning targets are\ngenerated from the available data themselves.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In <strong>reinforcement learning<\/strong>, the consequences of\nactions are evaluated through reward signals.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>This classification describes <strong>types of learning\nsignals<\/strong>. It is not a list of necessarily separate products. A\nlarge AI system can use multiple methods in succession or in\ncombination.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conventional programming rules versus learned\nrelationships<\/h2>\n<p>A traditional application can decide: ‘If the file extension is\n<code>.jpg<\/code>, treat the file as an image.’ This is an explicitly\nprogrammed rule. Another task is: ‘Which images show a dog?’ Size,\ncolours and shape are insufficient on their own. Learning from examples\nmay be useful here.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Conventional rule:\nInput → fixed rule formulated by a human → output\n\nLearned model:\nTraining data → learning procedure → model parameters\n                                         │\nNew input ───────────────────────────────┤\n                                         ▼\n                                 prediction/decision<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>In learning, the <strong>model parameters<\/strong> are the adjusted\nnumbers, such as the weights of a network. During subsequent use, these\nparameters are usually not changed with every request. Applying a\ntrained model is called <strong>inference<\/strong>. An image recognition\nprogram can therefore classify a thousand photos in succession without\nautomatically learning from them. A chat with a language model is\nlikewise not equivalent to retraining its weights. The differences are\ndiscussed in detail in M6-L2.<\/p>\n<p>ML is particularly attractive when there are many variations that are\ndifficult to capture using explicit rules. It is not, however,\nautomatically better for deterministic calculations. The VAT rule of a\nclearly defined billing system normally belongs in understandable\napplication code, not in a statistical prediction model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What a learning dataset consists of: Examples, features,\nlabels<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>dataset<\/strong> is initially just an organised collection\nof observations: measurements, images, texts, audio recordings, events\nor numbers that have already been processed. A <strong>training\nexample<\/strong> is a unit processed by a learning procedure, such as a\nphoto with accompanying information or a single row in a table.<\/p>\n<p>The descriptive inputs are called <strong>features<\/strong>. For the\nprice of a used car, these might be age, mileage and model. The desired\nresult is called the <strong>target value<\/strong> or\n<strong>label<\/strong>, for example, the actual sale price achieved. A\nlabel need not be a category: a number, a text passage or a measured\nevent can also serve as a learning target.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<th>Features<\/th>\n<th>Possible target<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Used car<\/td>\n<td>Year of manufacture, mileage, model<\/td>\n<td>Sale price<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Email<\/td>\n<td>Content, sender features<\/td>\n<td>‘Spam’ or ‘not spam’<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Photo<\/td>\n<td>Pixels, image region<\/td>\n<td>‘Dog’, ‘cat’, ‘other’<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Production plant<\/td>\n<td>Sensor readings over the last few minutes<\/td>\n<td>Fault in the next time window<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Rows in a table are not meaningful training data simply because there\nare many of them. They can contain measurement errors, duplicates,\nchance relationships or biased samples of reality. A common\nmisconception is already apparent here: <strong>more data do not\nautomatically mean better data.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For language models there is an additional term: a\n<strong>token<\/strong> is a unit into which a tokeniser breaks down\ntext. It may be a word, part of a word, a punctuation mark or another\nsequence of characters. Tokens are not semantic labels assigned by\nhumans. They serve as a computational representation and, depending on\nthe learning objective, also as prediction targets. Tokenisation is\ndiscussed in detail in M6-L1.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Supervised learning: Practising with examples that have\nknown answers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Supervised learning<\/strong> uses inputs with associated\ntarget values. The system is to learn a relationship that can also be\napplied to new, previously unseen inputs.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Training example A: Photo A + label &quot;cat&quot; ─┐\nTraining example B: Photo B + label &quot;dog&quot; ──┼→ model training\nTraining example C: Photo C + label &quot;dog&quot; ──┘       │\n                                                    ▼\n                                             learned parameters\n                                                    │\nNew photo (without known label) ────────────────────┘\n                                                    ▼\n                                             predicted class<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The two common basic tasks are <strong>classification<\/strong> and\n<strong>regression<\/strong>. Classification chooses a category or\nestimates probabilities for categories: is an email spam? Does an image\nbelong to ‘dog’ or ‘cat’? Regression estimates a numerical value: what\ntemperature is expected? Approximately how high is consumption?<\/p>\n<p>During training, the prediction is compared with the known target. A\n<strong>loss function<\/strong> expresses the deviation as a number.\nDepending on the model and optimisation procedure, the parameters are\nadjusted so that this loss decreases on training data. The computational\nidea from M5-L2 – forward pass, error, gradients, parameter changes –\napplies directly here. However, not every supervised ML method uses\nbackpropagation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Where do labels come from?<\/strong> They can be assigned by\nhumans, come from measurements, be derived from reliable business\nprocesses or be generated automatically using rules. Their origin is\ncrucial. If two people assess the same recording differently, this is\nnot a problem a network can simply make disappear. The model also learns\nthe ambiguities and inconsistencies in those labels.<\/p>\n<p>A well-fitting model recognises statistical patterns. It does not\nprove that its categories are philosophically unambiguous or that its\ndecisions are causally correct. A high value in a general test table is\ntherefore not sufficient for medical, legal or safety-critical\napplications.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Unsupervised learning: Structure without predefined target\nclasses<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>unsupervised learning<\/strong>, no separately supplied\ntarget value exists for each example. We look for recurring structures,\nsimilarities, groups or compact representations.<\/p>\n<p>An illustrative case is a music archive containing tracks for which\nno genre labels are stored. Tempo, acoustic features and other\nproperties might allow groups of similar tracks to be formed. A common\nmethod for this is <strong>clustering<\/strong>, the algorithmic\nformation of groups.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Many tracks with features\n        │\n        ▼\nDefine a similarity measure\n        │\n        ▼\nClustering procedure\n        │\n        ├── Group A: similar feature profiles\n        ├── Group B: different feature profiles\n        └── Group C: other similarities<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>In the familiar <strong>k-means method<\/strong>, a number\n<code>k<\/code> of groups is specified. The procedure assigns points\naccording to their proximity to group centres and repeatedly adjusts\nthese centres. This works only within the framework of the chosen\ndistance measure, feature representation and assumptions of the method.\nA group is not automatically a musically ‘true’ genre. A poorly chosen\nfeature can distort the grouping: files with the same bit rate might be\nclose together even though they have little in common musically.<\/p>\n<p>Other unsupervised applications include <strong>dimensionality\nreduction<\/strong> – replacing a large number of features with fewer\nsummarising numbers – and exploring unusual patterns. Not all anomaly\ndetection is unsupervised; some methods are trained specifically using\nlabelled failure cases.<\/p>\n<p>Unsupervised learning has an important limitation: without predefined\ntarget categories, a discovered grouping cannot simply be rated as ‘95\nper cent correct’. We examine the quality, stability, domain\nplausibility and usefulness of the groups for the actual purpose.\n<em>Unsupervised<\/em> therefore does not mean <em>without\nevaluation<\/em>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-supervised learning: When the data themselves provide\ntraining questions<\/h2>\n<p>For many tasks, human labelling of all examples would be\nunaffordable. Large quantities of text, for example, are available\nwithout tables specifying a desired answer for every sentence.\nNevertheless, they contain a natural structure: words and tokens occur\nin an order and in context.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Self-supervised learning<\/strong> uses this structure to\nderive learning objectives <strong>from the data themselves<\/strong>. It\nis a form of learning without external labels specified by humans for\nevery instance of the task. ‘Self’ does not mean that the model freely\nchooses its own objectives: developers define the training task.<\/p>\n<p>A simple example is predicting masked parts of a text:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Original:       &quot;The little dog sleeps in the garden.&quot;\nTraining text:  &quot;The little [MASKED] sleeps in the garden.&quot;\nLearning target: predict the original masked token<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The answer comes from the original text, not from a separately\ncreated label. This idea is used, among other things, in <strong>masked\nlanguage modelling<\/strong>. The research model <strong>BERT<\/strong>\nmade pretrained text representations using masked tokens an important\napproach.<\/p>\n<p>Another common training objective is predicting the <strong>next\ntoken<\/strong> in a sequence:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Observed sequence: &quot;The little dog&quot;\nDesired target:    next token in the original sequence<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Given the large number of possible continuations, the task can be\ndemanding. The model is therefore forced to learn many linguistic\nregularities. However, it does not automatically learn which statement\nis true in the outside world. A statistically plausible sentence can be\nfactually incorrect. This is precisely why token prediction must not be\nequated with verified factual knowledge.<\/p>\n<p>Self-supervised methods are not limited to language. Image models can\nreconstruct parts of an image or learn comparable representations from\ndifferently modified views of the same image. In audio and video,\nlearning signals can also be formed from sequences or partially masked\ncontent. The particular training objective determines which properties\nthe representation preferentially captures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reinforcement learning: Acting and learning from\nconsequences<\/h2>\n<p>So far, the training examples have essentially been predetermined\ndata. In <strong>reinforcement learning<\/strong> (<strong>RL<\/strong>),\nan acting system influences its next observation through its own\nactions. An <strong>agent<\/strong> selects an <strong>action<\/strong> in\nan <strong>environment<\/strong>. A new state or observation and a\n<strong>reward<\/strong> follow.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>            ┌───────────────────────┐\n            │ Agent                 │\n            │ Strategy / policy     │\n            └───────────┬───────────┘\n                        │ action\n                        ▼\n            ┌───────────────────────┐\n            │ Environment           │\n            │ e.g. game/simulation  │\n            └───────────┬───────────┘\n                        │ observation + reward\n                        └───────────────────► Agent<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The <strong>policy<\/strong> is the action strategy: which action\nshould be selected in an observed situation? The objective is typically\nnot just the reward of the next step, but a good <strong>cumulative\nreward<\/strong> over a sequence of steps. Sometimes it is worth\nsacrificing a short-term advantage to achieve a better result later.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exploration<\/strong> means also trying actions that are not\nyet well known. <strong>Exploitation<\/strong> means choosing an action\nthat has been promising so far. A system that exclusively repeats\nfamiliar actions may fail to learn a better strategy; a system that only\nexperiments may act inefficiently or dangerously.<\/p>\n<p>Example: A simulated warehouse vehicle receives points for correctly\nreached destinations and penalties for unnecessary journeys. It can test\ndifferent routes. But a poorly formulated reward can lead to absurd\nstrategies. If points are awarded only for ‘as many journeys as\npossible’, it might generate unnecessary trips. This phenomenon is\ncalled <strong>reward hacking<\/strong>: the system optimises the\nmeasurement signal rather than the intended objective.<\/p>\n<p>In real robotics, uncontrolled experimentation involves physical\nrisk. Developers therefore use simulation, safety rules, intervention\nlimits and conventional control engineering. Reinforcement learning is\nnot a licence for a language model to issue dangerous motor commands\ndirectly. The division of tasks between AI and safety-critical control\nfollows in M6-L7.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transfer learning: Bringing prior knowledge to a new\ntask<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>transfer learning<\/strong>, capabilities or\nrepresentations from an earlier learning phase are used for a new task.\nAn already trained image model can, for example, serve as the starting\npoint for recognising particular machine parts. It is then not\nnecessarily necessary to learn every visual structure from scratch.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Pretraining<\/strong> produces more generally reusable\nparameters. <strong>Fine-tuning<\/strong> adapts an existing model using\nfurther training data for a narrower purpose. Depending on the\nprocedure, all weights, only selected weights or additional small groups\nof parameters are adjusted.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Broad initial dataset\n           │\n           ▼\n    general pretraining\n           │\n           ▼\n    existing base model\n           │\n   additional suitable examples\n           ▼\n         fine-tuning\n           │\n           ▼\n    model for a narrower purpose<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Transfer learning is no guarantee: a model pretrained on ordinary\ndaylight images may be unsuitable for thermal images, medical imaging or\nsubstantially different production environments. It can inherit unwanted\nbiases from the source domain. And it differs from mere\n<strong>prompting<\/strong>: a new question or instruction does not\nitself change the stored model weights. These differences are examined\nsystematically in M6-L3.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why embeddings play a central role<\/h2>\n<p>Machines cannot directly process words, images or customers as\n‘meaning’. They need a numerical representation. One simple\nrepresentation of a category uses a separate position for every possible\nentry: <strong>one-hot encoding<\/strong>. With very many categories,\nsuch vectors become large and sparse; moreover, they express no\nproximity between similar concepts.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>embedding<\/strong> is a learned or otherwise computed\nrepresentation as a numerical vector in an often more compact space. The\nvector can capture properties that are useful for the training\nobjective. A greatly simplified, explicitly invented representation\nis:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>&quot;cat&quot;   → [0,8;  0,3; -0,1]\n&quot;dog&quot;   → [0,7;  0,2; -0,2]\n&quot;table&quot; → [-0,4; 0,9;  0,6]<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The three values are <strong>not real model values and not a fixed\nstandard of meaning<\/strong>. In real models, vectors can have many\ndimensions. Under a particular model and training definition, their\ndistance or angle may express something about similarity. This is useful\nfor search systems, recommendations and later retrieval-augmented\ngeneration (RAG) systems.<\/p>\n<p>Three limitations are important. First, ‘similarity’ depends on the\nlearning objective: the German word ‘Bank’ can mean a financial\ninstitution or a bench. Second, a suitable vector does not itself\nproduce a reliable logical conclusion. Third, embeddings can contain\nsensitive attributes or allow sensitive inferences; storing them is not\nautomatically harmless. M6-L1 shows how token embeddings become\ntransformer inputs, and M6-L4 explains their role in document\nsearch.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The training process: Not one example, but many passes<\/h2>\n<p>Typical training starts long before the first weight change. Data\nmust be selected, prepared, checked and converted into a numerical space\nsuitable for the model. In supervised learning, repeated comparisons\nwith target values follow.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Data sources\n    │\n    ▼\nCheck rights, quality and purpose\n    │\n    ▼\nSeparate training, validation and test data appropriately\n    │\n    ▼\nPrepare features/tokens\n    │\n    ▼\nModel training on training data\n    │\n    ├─ prediction\n    ├─ calculate loss\n    ├─ adjust parameters\n    └─ next examples\n    │\n    ▼\nModel selection using validation data\n    │\n    ▼\nFinal test on untouched test data\n    │\n    ▼\nOperation, monitoring and retraining if necessary<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>In neural networks, examples are frequently processed in\n<strong>mini-batches<\/strong>: groups of multiple examples from which an\noptimisation step is calculated. An <strong>epoch<\/strong> usually means\nthat the examples designated for a training round have been processed\nonce. Not every training setup is epoch-based; other counting methods\nare common with continuous data streams or very large text\ncollections.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>optimiser<\/strong> chooses how weights are changed on the\nbasis of the error. The <strong>learning rate<\/strong> influences the\nstep size. <strong>Hyperparameters<\/strong> are settings of the learning\nprocedure or architecture – for example, learning rate, batch size or\nnumber of layers – and in ordinary training are not themselves learned\nin the same way as the model weights.<\/p>\n<p>Example with freely chosen numbers: with 1,000 training examples and\na batch size of 100, ten batches would be needed for a complete pass,\nprovided no special remainder or omission of examples has to be taken\ninto account. Five such epochs would correspond to 50 batch passes. This\nis a <strong>numerical illustration<\/strong>, not a recommendation for a\nparticular model. Large models may use completely different data\nvolumes, batch definitions and training schedules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why training, validation and test data are separated<\/h2>\n<p>Good training performance does not prove that the model can handle\nnew situations. A person can memorise an examination answer and still\nnot master the subject. Similarly, a model can reproduce many training\nexamples without responding usefully to new data. We therefore\ndistinguish between:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_033_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Training and validation error in possible overfitting\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Training error continues to decrease while\nvalidation error rises after reaching a minimum: a schematic sign of\noverfitting.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_034_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three separate datasets for training, model selection and final evaluation, with schematic training and validation error curves below.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Training data fit model parameters, validation data\nguide model selection, and held-out test data support final\nevaluation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Training, validation and testing have different roles; the error\ncurves are schematic.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Training data:<\/strong> Used to learn parameters.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Validation data:<\/strong> Used to compare variants and\nhyperparameters during development.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Test data:<\/strong> Preferably left untouched until the\nfinal evaluation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>An often illustrated split is 70/15/15; it is <strong>not a law of\nnature or a general requirement<\/strong>. An appropriate split depends\non data volume, time dependencies, rare events and the actual\napplication.<\/p>\n<p>A photo archive may contain twenty almost identical photos of the\nsame animal. If fifteen enter training and five enter testing, the\nevaluation may mainly measure recognition of that photo series. For\nrecognising unfamiliar animals, it would be better not to distribute\nrelated image series or animals across both datasets. In time series, a\ntest period from the future should often not be mixed with information\nthat was not yet available at the time of an earlier prediction.<\/p>\n<p>This problem is called <strong>data leakage<\/strong>: information\nfrom the subsequent test or from the future inadvertently enters\ntraining or selection. Even normalisation that calculates its mean from\n<em>all<\/em> examples, including the test, can already leak information.\nThe same applies if, after observing a disappointing test result,\nimprovements are made until the test looks good. The supposedly\nindependent test has then effectively become part of development.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Generalisation<\/strong> means that a model works reliably on\nnew examples from the intended application domain. This must be\ndemonstrated with realistically separated data; it does not follow\nautomatically from model size or low training error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A numerical example: Why 99 per cent accuracy can be\nmisleading<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a service must identify the ten genuinely fraudulent cases\namong 1,000 fictional transactions. A completely useless system that\nreports everything as non-suspicious is <strong>correct 990\ntimes<\/strong>, giving <strong>99 per cent accuracy<\/strong>. However,\nit detects <strong>not a single case of fraud<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Now consider another, also entirely invented model:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Actual case<\/th>\n<th>Model reports ‘fraud’<\/th>\n<th>Model reports ‘no fraud’<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fraud (10 cases)<\/td>\n<td>8 true positives<\/td>\n<td>2 false negatives<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No fraud (990 cases)<\/td>\n<td>12 false positives<\/td>\n<td>978 true negatives<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The four cells form a <strong>confusion matrix<\/strong>. Various\nmeasures can be calculated from it:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Accuracy:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>978<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>98<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{8+978}{1\\,000}=98{,}6\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Precision:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>8<\/mn><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac8{8+12}=40\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOf all cases flagged as suspicious, 40 per cent are actually\nfraudulent.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Recall:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>8<\/mn><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac8{8+2}=80\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOf all genuine fraud cases, the model finds 80 per cent.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>F1 score:<\/strong> harmonic mean of precision and recall;\nhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>53<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{2\\cdot0{,}4\\cdot0{,}8}{0{,}4+0{,}8}\\approx53{,}3\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The second model has <strong>lower accuracy<\/strong> than the useless\nfirst one, but detects eight out of ten relevant cases. Which solution\nis better depends on the cost of missed fraud and false alarms. False\nalarms may unfairly burden people; missed cases can cause damage. Human\nreview processes and appropriate thresholds are therefore also needed.\n<strong>A single evaluation metric never tells the whole\nstory.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>In regression, other metrics are considered, such as mean absolute\nerror. For language models, these include prediction losses,\ntask-specific tests and human ratings. A language model that achieves a\ngood value on its training objective therefore need not be able to\ncalculate reliably or reproduce every source correctly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Benchmarks and the difficulty of fair comparisons<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>benchmark<\/strong> is a standardised test or collection of\ntasks intended to make models comparable. Benchmarks are helpful because\nmere advertising claims such as ‘intelligent’ or ‘understands\neverything’ are not verifiable performance descriptions. A benchmark\nscore, however, applies only under its specific conditions.<\/p>\n<p>Evaluation questions include: which data were used? Is the test\nrepresentative? Which languages and user groups occur? Were solutions or\nnear duplicates already used in training? How many attempts was the\nsystem allowed? Were external tools used? Was computing time\nlimited?<\/p>\n<p>If a benchmark is repeatedly consulted during development, a model\ncan be indirectly tailored to it. The score then loses some of its\nability to describe performance on genuinely unseen cases. This applies\nboth to conventional ML models and to large language models.<\/p>\n<p>Important failure modes are <strong>overfitting<\/strong>,\n<strong>underfitting<\/strong> and <strong>distribution shift<\/strong>.\nThe latter arises when new cases differ from the data on which the model\nlearned or was tested: different cameras, changed language, new\nprocedures, a changed customer base or rare exceptional situations.<\/p>\n<p>Particularly in operation, <strong>training-serving skew<\/strong> can\noccur: data are processed differently during training than during actual\ninference. If a prediction service interprets dates as days in training\nand as seconds in operation, for example, well-trained parameters do not\nhelp. Different model versions, missing values and shifted data\ndistributions must therefore be monitored.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What does learning mean – and what does it explicitly not\nmean?<\/h2>\n<p>The term ‘learning’ easily leads to misconceptions. A neural network\ndoes not collect human experiences from which understanding\nautomatically arises. It adjusts numbers according to an optimisation\nobjective. Conventional ML methods may involve other forms of parameter\nestimation or rule formation; nevertheless, the objective remains\nmathematically defined.<\/p>\n<p>Three distinctions are particularly important:<\/p>\n<p><strong>Correlation is not causation.<\/strong> If many heating system\nfailures occur in winter in training data, a model may use the season as\na strong indicator. It does not follow that the calendar causes the\nfailure. The actual causes could be temperature, maintenance or load. If\nthe system is used in a different environment, the relationship may\ndisappear.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Pattern recognition is not automatically factual\nknowledge.<\/strong> A language model can confidently complete a common\nphrase even though the statement is false. Linguistic fluency and\nfactual correctness are different criteria.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Feedback during use is not automatically training.<\/strong> A\nstar rating, thumbs-up or corrected chat text may serve as a subsequent\ntraining signal in a separate process, depending on the application and\ndata policy. The current inference, however, initially computes using\nthe existing model parameters.<\/p>\n<p>These distinctions help avoid exaggerated expectations and plan\nappropriate control measures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three practical examples using different learning\nmethods<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"example-a-local-photo-and-media-archive\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example A: Local photo\nand media archive<\/h3>\n<p>A local archive contains many images that are to be searchable by\nsimilarity. Instead of defining all categories beforehand, an already\ntrained image model could generate <strong>embeddings<\/strong> for the\nimages. A search tool then finds nearby vectors. For automatically\nassigning user-defined categories, some labelled examples and a\nsupervised model could additionally be useful.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Private image files → local image encoder → embedding vectors\n                                                │\nQuery image ────────→ local image encoder ──────┤\n                                                ▼\n                                          similar images<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>When is less sufficient?<\/strong> If only filenames,\nlocations and simple tags are searched, a conventional local index is\nmuch easier to operate. An AI model requires computing time, storage\nspace and appropriate usage rights. For private photographs, locally\nprocessed information is often easier to control than an unreviewed\nexternal upload. The system must also make false similarities and\nmisleading categories apparent.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-b-small-online-analytical-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example B: Small\nonline analytical service<\/h3>\n<p>A service is to detect whether technical measurements indicate an\nimminent device failure. If reliable fault logs exist, a\n<strong>supervised classifier<\/strong> is a natural option. Without such\nlabels, anomaly detection can draw attention to unusual patterns, but\ncannot prove that these patterns are genuine failures.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Device measurements → cleaning → model → risk indication\n                         │                  │\n                         │                  ▼\n                         │              human review\n                         ▼\n               training + evaluation\n               on separate data<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Measurement data from the same device and the same time must not be\nscattered across testing and training in a way that artificially\nimproves evaluation. A model change requires versioning, monitoring and\nthe ability to return to a proven version. False alarms consume\nattention; missed failures can cause costly interruptions. Operation\ntherefore remains a <strong>software and organisational\nproblem<\/strong>, not merely a question of neural networks.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-c-language-model-for-documents-and-ai-jobs\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example C:\nLanguage model for documents and AI jobs<\/h3>\n<p>A provider has many documents whose content it wants to make\naccessible through a language model. A large language model may\npreviously have been <strong>pretrained<\/strong> on very many text\nsequences using self-supervised token prediction. It does not need to be\ntrained from scratch again for business document search.<\/p>\n<p>A leaner solution can connect an existing model to a documented\nsearch function: an index finds relevant passages and passes them to the\nmodel to formulate an answer. This procedure is called\n<em>retrieval-augmented generation<\/em> (<strong>RAG<\/strong>) and is\nexplained in detail in M6-L4. It does not automatically change the model\nweights.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Own documents → authorised index ──────┐\n                                       ▼\nUser question → search → relevant passages → existing language model\n                                               │\n                                               ▼\n                                     answer with source references<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The architecture must respect document access rights, check consent\nor valid legal bases, limit storage and API costs, and make the source\nattribution visible. Fine-tuning would be a different decision requiring\njustification; it guarantees neither the currency of documents nor the\ncorrectness of individual answers. For small collections, even\nconventional full-text search may be the best solution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Costs, environment, rights and responsibility in\ntraining<\/h2>\n<p>Training consumes resources: computing time on a <strong>Central\nProcessing Unit (CPU)<\/strong> or <strong>Graphics Processing Unit\n(GPU)<\/strong>, working memory or graphics memory, data transfer,\nstorage media, electricity and cooling. How expensive a procedure is\ndepends enormously on model size, data volume, algorithm, hardware and\nrepetitions. A small tabular model may be trainable locally in a short\ntime; pretraining a large language model is on an entirely different\nscale. The term ‘AI’ alone cannot yield <strong>credible figures for\ncosts or energy use<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>There are also indirect costs: checking data quality, correcting\nerroneous labels, managing model versions, performing tests, explaining\nresults, handling complaints and investigating unwanted decisions. Then\nthere is ongoing <strong>inference<\/strong> – even an already trained\nmodel consumes resources whenever it is used. The economic question is\ntherefore not only ‘How much does training cost?’, but also ‘What\ndemonstrated benefit does a correctly handled case deliver?’ The general\ncost models from M4-L4 still apply.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Data protection:<\/strong> Training data may contain personal\ninformation. Depending on the processing, the General Data Protection\nRegulation (GDPR) requires, among other things, a valid legal basis,\npurpose limitation, data minimisation and suitable safeguards. Publicly\naccessible data are not exempt from data protection requirements merely\nbecause they are public. An embedding can also reveal information about\na person; numerical form alone does not automatically make data\nanonymous.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Copyright and usage rights:<\/strong> Permission to store data\ndoes not automatically mean that every kind of model training with those\ndata is permitted. Contracts, database rights, licences, copyright rules\nand any exceptions for text and data mining must be assessed separately.\nThe specific legal questions depend on the jurisdiction, type of data\nand use; this lesson is not legal advice.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Fairness and representativeness:<\/strong> If certain user\ngroups, dialects or devices are barely represented in training data,\nmodel performance for these groups may be considerably worse. Sensitive\napplications therefore require appropriate subgroup analyses and careful\ndomain-specific review. A formally high average score does not eliminate\nthis problem.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Security and maintenance:<\/strong> Data and models are\nvaluable resources. Training data can be manipulated (<em>data\npoisoning<\/em>), stored models compromised, interfaces abused and\nconfidential content inadvertently disclosed. Access, sources, model\nversions and recovery procedures must therefore be protected. This\nresponsibility disappears neither through self-supervised learning nor\nthrough the use of an external model API.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which type of learning fits when – and when does none\nfit?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Objective<\/th>\n<th>Natural approach<\/th>\n<th>Typical difficulty<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Predict a known category or number<\/td>\n<td>Supervised learning<\/td>\n<td>Good labels, representative data<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Group similar objects<\/td>\n<td>Unsupervised learning<\/td>\n<td>Choosing useful features and groups<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Learn general representations from raw text<\/td>\n<td>Self-supervised learning<\/td>\n<td>Large data and computing volumes, quality of the learning\nobjective<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Optimise the consequences of actions<\/td>\n<td>Reinforcement learning<\/td>\n<td>Safe exploration, reward design<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Specialise existing model capabilities<\/td>\n<td>Transfer learning / fine-tuning<\/td>\n<td>Differences between source and target domains<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apply an exact calculation rule<\/td>\n<td>Conventional program code<\/td>\n<td>Model and test the rule correctly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Search one’s own text files<\/td>\n<td>Full-text search, possibly RAG<\/td>\n<td>Rights, currency, source attribution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This table is a <strong>decision aid<\/strong>, not a fixed\nassignment. An image model can, for example, be pretrained using\nself-supervised learning and then fine-tuned under supervision. A\nrobotics system can combine learned perception, a trained movement\nstrategy and fixed programmed safety limits.<\/p>\n<p>Especially for small projects, ask about the problem first, then\nchoose the technology. A good search box, an SQL index, a clear user\ninterface or a simple statistical analysis may provide more practical\nvalue than a specially trained neural network.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M6-L1<\/h2>\n<p>Machines do not learn in just one way. The decisive factor is\n<strong>which signal controls a model change<\/strong>: known target\nvalues, unlabelled structures, prediction tasks derived from the data or\nthe consequences of actions. Transfer learning can reuse previously\ntrained parameters. This explains why modern AI systems are often\ndeveloped in multiple stages: pretraining on broad data, adapting to a\ntask, testing separately and then deploying in a controlled manner.<\/p>\n<p>Every learning procedure involves questions of data quality,\ncomputing requirements, fair evaluation, leakage, security and usage\nrights. Embeddings connect data with numerical spaces; benchmarks help\nwith comparison, but do not replace realistic evaluation. Good training\nscores are not yet a promise that a model understands the world causally\nor behaves reliably under new conditions.<\/p>\n<p>The next lesson, <strong>M6-L1 – From language processing to\ntransformers and LLMs<\/strong>, shows how tokens, embeddings and\nsequence models lead to the transformer architecture. Building on this,\nM6-L2 explains step by step how a large language model calculates its\noutput during a conversation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Checked on 04.11.2026.<\/strong> The linked sources are\nprimary documentation and original research; the examples, diagrams,\nmetrics and illustrative training data in this lesson were independently\nproduced. The fictional case numbers given are not model benchmarks or\nprovider performance figures.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, Machine Learning Crash Course.<\/strong> Official\nintroduction to ML tasks, datasets, classification, neural networks,\nembeddings and model operation: <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, ‘Datasets: Dividing the original dataset’.<\/strong>\nTraining, validation and test sets, separation and generalisation: <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/dividing-datasets\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/dividing-datasets<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>scikit-learn, ‘Common pitfalls and recommended\npractices’.<\/strong> Data leakage, preprocessing, cross-validation and\nsafe test separation: <a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html\">https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, ‘Introduction to clustering’.<\/strong> Unsupervised\nclustering, similarity measures and k-means: <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/clustering\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/clustering<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Hugging Face, ‘Masked language modeling’.<\/strong> The\nlearning objective of predicting masked tokens from textual context: <a\nhref=\"https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/tasks/masked_language_modeling\">https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/tasks/masked_language_modeling<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Devlin, Chang, Lee and Toutanova (2018), ‘BERT: Pre-training\nof Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding’.<\/strong>\nOriginal research on pretrained text representations: <a\nhref=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.04805\">https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.04805<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>OpenAI, ‘Spinning Up – Key Concepts and\nTerminology’.<\/strong> Agent, environment, actions, policy, reward and\ncumulative reward: <a\nhref=\"https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/spinningup/rl_intro.html\">https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/spinningup/rl_intro.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, ‘Embeddings’.<\/strong> Numerical vectors, one-hot\nencoding and limitations of large sparse representations: <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/embeddings\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/embeddings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">9.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, ‘Embeddings: Obtaining embeddings’.<\/strong> Learned\nvectors, static and contextual representations: <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/embeddings/obtaining-embeddings\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/embeddings/obtaining-embeddings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">10.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, ‘Classification: Accuracy, recall, precision, and\nrelated metrics’.<\/strong> Definitions and limitations of classification\nmetrics: <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy-precision-recall\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy-precision-recall<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">11.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, ‘Production ML systems: Monitoring\npipelines’.<\/strong> Checking for training-serving skew and changes in\nproduction: <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/production-ml-systems/monitoring\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/production-ml-systems/monitoring<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">12.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Google, ‘Production ML systems: When to transform\ndata?’.<\/strong> Comparison of preprocessing during training and\ninference: <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/production-ml-systems/transforming-data\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/production-ml-systems/transforming-data<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Scope:<\/strong> The technical teaching sources do not\nestablish a specific data protection assessment for an individual case.\nLegal obligations require a separate review of the particular project\nand the applicable regulations.<\/p>\n<\/article>"};
